WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:02.250
INTRO:
We always heard about billionaires who dropped

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:05.230
out of college to launch companies and hit
it big.

00:00:05.230 --> 00:00:09.580
Everyone from Bill Gates and Mark Zuckerberg
to Steve Jobs and Michael Dell.

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:13.890
In fact, the billionaire’s list is disproportionately
made up of college dropouts.

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:18.550
But, while these are insane success stories,
the one similarity between all of these stories

00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:22.460
is that they happened in western countries
and more specifically America.

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:27.359
Not only does this make the journey itself
easier given that there is more opportunity,

00:00:27.359 --> 00:00:33.030
but more importantly, the consequences of
failing are actually not that bad.

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:37.789
Worst-case scenario, you can just flip burgers
at McDonald’s and live paycheck to paycheck.

00:00:37.789 --> 00:00:42.050
In many Asian countries, however, if you don’t
make it with your business and you don’t

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:46.739
have a degree, you could very well end up
homeless given the extreme competition for

00:00:46.739 --> 00:00:48.219
even entry-level jobs.

00:00:48.219 --> 00:00:52.530
Despite this, an Indian man named Gautam Adani
was not only willing to drop out of college

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:55.909
but also willing to drop out of high school
to chase his dreams.

00:00:55.909 --> 00:01:00.449
And, just last week, Gautam surpassed Mukesh
Ambani to become Asia’s richest person.

00:01:00.449 --> 00:01:06.560
So, here’s how a high school dropout became
Asia’s richest man.

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:11.330
DROPOUT:
Taking a look back, Gautam was born on June

00:01:11.330 --> 00:01:14.600
24, 1962, in Ahmedabad, India.

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:18.220
His father was a local textile merchant which
should’ve provided Gautam with a decent

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:19.780
upbringing financially.

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:24.410
But the problem was that Gautam had 7 siblings,
so his father’s average income didn’t

00:01:24.410 --> 00:01:25.410
stretch that far.

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:29.280
While his family wasn’t particularly rich,
his father’s small business likely swayed

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:31.060
the core values of the family.

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:36.070
Generally, in India, college is thought of
as the holy grail, and people who don’t

00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:38.840
follow the traditional path are strongly stigmatized
within society.

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:43.930
But, it looks like Gautam’s father’s business
made the family more open to alternative paths.

00:01:43.930 --> 00:01:48.050
And this was quite important given that Gautam
hated school from the very beginning.

00:01:48.050 --> 00:01:51.610
He had very little interest in academics and
he didn’t feel that school could get him

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:53.290
to where he wanted to be anyway.

00:01:53.290 --> 00:01:58.450
So, at age 16, he dropped out of high school
and moved to Mumbai to give business a shot.

00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:01.770
He only had a few hundred rupees in his pocket
or less than a hundred dollars.

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:05.740
It’s not clear what exactly he tried to
do in Mumbai, but it seems like it was a big

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:10.360
failure as Gautam would return home and enroll
in Gujurat University which is known for having

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:11.599
extremely generous admissions.

00:02:11.599 --> 00:02:15.439
They don’t post a formal acceptance rate,
so it’s thought that anybody who applies

00:02:15.439 --> 00:02:17.970
and meets the base criteria is accepted.

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:21.200
In other words, Gautam basically enrolled
in a community college.

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:24.799
This didn’t matter much though as Gautam
would drop out of here as well just two years

00:02:24.799 --> 00:02:26.590
later at age 18.

00:02:26.590 --> 00:02:30.359
He traveled to Mumbai once again with a few
hundred more rupees, but this time he had

00:02:30.359 --> 00:02:31.359
a plan.

00:02:31.359 --> 00:02:35.359
Gautam went ahead and scored an entry-level
job as a diamond sorter at an exporting company

00:02:35.359 --> 00:02:39.129
called Mahendra Brothers, not to be confused
with Mahindra Motors.

00:02:39.129 --> 00:02:43.109
While this job didn’t pay much, it gave
Gautam insight into the diamond business and

00:02:43.109 --> 00:02:46.230
he would use this knowledge to start up a
diamond flipping business.

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:49.790
This turned out to be quite successful and
made Guatam a good amount of money.

00:02:49.790 --> 00:02:53.319
It’s not like he could just retire or anything,
but he no longer had to worry about making

00:02:53.319 --> 00:02:54.319
ends meet.

00:02:54.319 --> 00:02:57.959
In the meantime, one of his brothers who was
also inclined towards business started up

00:02:57.959 --> 00:03:01.379
a small plastics factory and asked Gautam
to come join him.

00:03:01.379 --> 00:03:05.590
Given that Gautam didn’t want to flip diamonds
for the rest of his life, he took on the offer,

00:03:05.590 --> 00:03:08.419
but he never went all-in on his brother’s
business.

00:03:08.419 --> 00:03:12.999
You see, the plastics business was rather
cutthroat with small profit margins and significant

00:03:12.999 --> 00:03:13.999
competition.

00:03:13.999 --> 00:03:18.390
So, Gautam didn’t want to build a plastics
company, but Gautam did quickly recognize

00:03:18.390 --> 00:03:20.540
a greater opportunity within the industry.

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:25.139
One of the most important materials required
for plastic production is PVC, and it seemed

00:03:25.139 --> 00:03:28.599
to Gautam that all of their PVC suppliers
were fairing much better than them.

00:03:28.599 --> 00:03:33.370
So, Gautam traveled to South Korea to meet
with PVC producers and make an importing deal.

00:03:33.370 --> 00:03:37.469
And with that, Gautam launched Adani Exports
limited in 1988.

00:03:37.469 --> 00:03:42.419
THE ADANI GROUP:
Gautam started the company with a total of

00:03:42.419 --> 00:03:46.379
500,000 rupees, which at the time was equivalent
to about $36,000.

00:03:46.379 --> 00:03:52.189
Initially, the company imported PVC and sold
it to local plastic factories, but it didn’t

00:03:52.189 --> 00:03:54.620
take long for Gautam to expand outside this.

00:03:54.620 --> 00:03:59.359
Beginning in 1991, the Indian government changed
their attitude on business and started passing

00:03:59.359 --> 00:04:02.090
legislation that was much more favorable to
companies.

00:04:02.090 --> 00:04:06.890
For example, they lightened licensing restrictions,
lowered tariffs, and loosened banking policies.

00:04:06.890 --> 00:04:11.419
This period has since become known as the
economic liberalization of India.

00:04:11.419 --> 00:04:15.900
All these policies led to an influx of capital
into the Indian economy and allowed businesses

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:19.810
to expand faster than ever before, and this
was perfect timing for Gautam.

00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:23.910
Gautam went ahead and leveraged these policies
to expand the company into trading metals,

00:04:23.910 --> 00:04:25.950
textiles, and agricultural commodities.

00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:30.560
A few years later, India opened up the Mundra
shipping port to private companies, and in

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:32.440
1995, Gautum would win the contract.

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:36.610
Since then, the Mundra port has become the
largest private port in India.

00:04:36.610 --> 00:04:39.920
Gautam quickly followed up this success by
opening up a power plant in 1996.

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:45.600
And by 1998, the Adani Group became India’s
largest net foreign exchange earner.

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:49.390
Despite all this success, Gautam was yet to
even enter his most successful industry which

00:04:49.390 --> 00:04:50.940
was the coal industry.

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:55.770
The coal industry has historically had a high
barrier to entry given the massive costs associated

00:04:55.770 --> 00:04:58.480
with starting up and the monopolistic nature
of the business.

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:02.310
However, on the flip side, if you are able
to dethrone the current coal leader, you’d

00:05:02.310 --> 00:05:03.980
likely be set for decades.

00:05:03.980 --> 00:05:08.100
Gautam was well aware of this, so he didn’t
enter this market until he was truly ready

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:09.960
which wasn’t till 1999.

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:14.130
Soon after, Gautam formed a partnership with
a leading Singaporean company called the Wilmar

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:15.130
Group.

00:05:15.130 --> 00:05:18.560
Together they launched a joint venture called
Adani Wilmar, and this cemented Gautam’s

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:20.060
position within the big leagues.

00:05:20.060 --> 00:05:24.570
But, when you start playing in the big leagues,
you start dealing with the big problems.

00:05:24.570 --> 00:05:29.280
DEATH’S DOOR:
One of the biggest concerns for many billionaires

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:32.520
is being assassinated and/or being held for
ransom.

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:36.740
This is why many CEOs and billionaires spend
millions if not tens of millions every year

00:05:36.740 --> 00:05:37.750
on security.

00:05:37.750 --> 00:05:42.060
They also have many odd practices to reduce
the likelihood of being assassinated.

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:46.310
Donald Trump, for example, is one of the most
flamboyant billionaires out there.

00:05:46.310 --> 00:05:50.590
You would think that he loves fine dining
and fancy restaurants, but more times than

00:05:50.590 --> 00:05:53.150
not, Trump actually prefers to eat at fast-food
restaurants.

00:05:53.150 --> 00:05:57.460
He feels that if he randomly shows up at a
fast-food restaurant, there’s a much smaller

00:05:57.460 --> 00:06:01.840
likelihood of getting poisoned than if he
made a reservation at a fancy restaurant.

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:05.750
This is truly quite ironic, but it’s part
of the price you often have to pay to get

00:06:05.750 --> 00:06:06.750
to the top.

00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:10.710
It’s not clear how much Gautam paid attention
to such threats, but it quickly became clear

00:06:10.710 --> 00:06:12.760
that he should’ve paid a lot more attention.

00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:17.940
In 1998, as Gautam and one of his friends,
Shantilal Patel, exited the Karnavati Club,

00:06:17.940 --> 00:06:20.720
there were stopped at gunpoint by a group
of men.

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:24.400
They forced the duo to board a van and they
took them to an undisclosed location.

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:27.110
Fortunately, the group wasn’t looking to
kill Gautam.

00:06:27.110 --> 00:06:31.670
They just wanted his money, and as soon as
he handed over $2 million, they were set free.

00:06:31.670 --> 00:06:36.000
The kidnapping was eventually linked to two
gangsters named Fazl-ur-Rehman and Bhogilal

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:38.390
Darji both of whom had extensive criminal
histories.

00:06:38.390 --> 00:06:42.640
I’m sure Gautam was a lot more cautious
following this incident, but ten years later,

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:45.830
he would find himself in yet another close
death encounter.

00:06:45.830 --> 00:06:49.720
This time though, it wasn’t on the streets
of India, it was in one of the most luxurious

00:06:49.720 --> 00:06:52.900
and bougie places in India: The Taj Hotel.

00:06:52.900 --> 00:06:58.430
On November 26, 2008, the Taj Hotel was one
of the targets of a series of terrorist attacks.

00:06:58.430 --> 00:07:02.700
A group of 10 terrorists invaded the Taj Hotel,
killing dozens of people and taking the rest

00:07:02.700 --> 00:07:04.640
of the hotel as hostage.

00:07:04.640 --> 00:07:09.270
Unfortunately, Gautam was one of the people
at the Taj Hotel when this attack took place,

00:07:09.270 --> 00:07:12.610
and he says that he witnessed death from a
distance of 15 feet.

00:07:12.610 --> 00:07:16.200
Fortunately, though, the hotel staff heroically
led the surviving victims of the attack to

00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:19.700
the basement and eventually a chamber hall
where to took refuge.

00:07:19.700 --> 00:07:23.900
They stayed here for the next 2.5 days until
India’s National Security Guards invaded

00:07:23.900 --> 00:07:27.560
the Taj and took out the terrorists on November
29, 2008.

00:07:27.560 --> 00:07:30.920
Evidently, this was the worst experience of
Gautam’s life.

00:07:30.920 --> 00:07:34.250
It did give him a new perspective on life
though which he has carried with him ever

00:07:34.250 --> 00:07:35.620
since.

00:07:35.620 --> 00:07:40.550
CONTROVERSIAL HISTORY:
So far, we’ve covered the bright side of

00:07:40.550 --> 00:07:44.010
Gautam, but many would argue that Gautam also
has a dark side.

00:07:44.010 --> 00:07:48.090
Historically, Gautam has been notorious for
leveraging his wealth and power to gain even

00:07:48.090 --> 00:07:49.300
more wealth and power.

00:07:49.300 --> 00:07:53.790
For example, you guys may be familiar with
the prime minister of India, Narendra Modi.

00:07:53.790 --> 00:07:57.750
Recently, he’s become one of the most influential
figures in international politics.

00:07:57.750 --> 00:08:00.860
But, Gautam has actually been supporting Modi
since 2003.

00:08:00.860 --> 00:08:06.140
So, there’s no doubt that Gautam is within
the good graces of Modi, and many critics

00:08:06.140 --> 00:08:10.710
have suggested that Gautam has used this connection
to influence national politics and pass beneficial

00:08:10.710 --> 00:08:12.350
legislation for himself.

00:08:12.350 --> 00:08:16.690
Aside from strategic lobbying, by far the
biggest criticism against Gautam is his environmental

00:08:16.690 --> 00:08:17.690
impact.

00:08:17.690 --> 00:08:21.010
Given that he made a lot of his money from
coal, his business is naturally going to have

00:08:21.010 --> 00:08:22.580
a negative environmental impact.

00:08:22.580 --> 00:08:25.900
But, this is not actually what critics are
talking about.

00:08:25.900 --> 00:08:30.160
Critics generally point to instances in which
Gautam outrightly defies environmental policies.

00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:35.440
For example, in 2011, Gautam’s company approved
an unseaworthy ship carrying coal for a voyage

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:36.440
across the ocean.

00:08:36.440 --> 00:08:40.650
But, the ship ended up sinking which caused
60,000 metric tonnes of coal to be deposited

00:08:40.650 --> 00:08:41.800
into the ocean.

00:08:41.800 --> 00:08:46.160
Gautam never did anything to clean up the
mess and simply paid a $975,000 fine.

00:08:46.160 --> 00:08:51.830
Similarly, in 2010, his company caused a pollution
disaster in Zambia that ended up poisoning

00:08:51.830 --> 00:08:53.130
the Kafue River.

00:08:53.130 --> 00:08:57.980
And in 2016, his company started work on their
Hazira Port without government approval which

00:08:57.980 --> 00:09:00.610
made it impossible for local families to fish.

00:09:00.610 --> 00:09:05.240
On top of his environmental impact, many argue
that Gautam is having a negative social impact

00:09:05.240 --> 00:09:06.250
as well.

00:09:06.250 --> 00:09:10.750
For example, he worked with the police to
intimate people off their land, and he’s

00:09:10.750 --> 00:09:12.840
accused of exploiting workers.

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:17.320
Many of his low-level miners and construction
workers are paid less than $4 per day.

00:09:17.320 --> 00:09:23.740
So, clearly, Gautam is by no means a saint,
but he is trying to turn the tide.

00:09:23.740 --> 00:09:27.610
REFORM:
While Gautam is by no means shutting down

00:09:27.610 --> 00:09:31.380
his coal operations, he has done significant
good deeds in other sectors.

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:36.610
For example, he opened free schools across
India which have provided education to 100,000

00:09:36.610 --> 00:09:37.690
underprivileged children.

00:09:37.690 --> 00:09:41.740
Recently, he has also been trying to combat
his negative environmental impact.

00:09:41.740 --> 00:09:45.950
Last summer, Gautam pledged to invest $10
billion into green energy within the next

00:09:45.950 --> 00:09:46.950
three years.

00:09:46.950 --> 00:09:51.500
And that’s just a fraction of his commitment
to invest $70 billion into green energy by

00:09:51.500 --> 00:09:52.500
2030.

00:09:52.500 --> 00:09:56.080
This of course doesn’t negate all of his
damage from the past, but it is nice to see

00:09:56.080 --> 00:09:59.430
that Gautam is being sensitive to his environmental
impact moving forward.

00:09:59.430 --> 00:10:04.130
In terms of wealth, Gautam reached a peak
net worth of $88.5 billion when he overtook

00:10:04.130 --> 00:10:05.360
Mukesh.

00:10:05.360 --> 00:10:10.290
Gautam has since fallen back slightly below
Mukesh, but given that Gautam is up $10 billion

00:10:10.290 --> 00:10:14.990
on the year and Mukesh is down $1 billion
on the year, it looks like it’s just a matter

00:10:14.990 --> 00:10:19.770
of time until Gautam decisively overtakes
Mukesh, but that’s just what I think.

00:10:19.770 --> 00:10:24.110
Do you guys think Gautam got lucky or do you
think he can do it again today without a college

00:10:24.110 --> 00:10:25.110
degree?

00:10:25.110 --> 00:10:26.110
Comment that down below.

00:10:26.110 --> 00:10:29.780
Also, drop a like if you think that people
without degrees shouldn’t be stigmatized.

00:10:29.780 --> 00:10:33.810
And of course, consider checking out our international
channels to watch our videos in other languages

00:10:33.810 --> 00:10:44.540
and consider subscribing to see more questions
logically answered.

